  "summary": "Korean dak paper strong enough for floors, armor and lamps — a thousand sheets from one tree.",
  "description": "Hanji is paper from the inner bark of the paper mulberry (dak), beaten into long fibers and couched on bamboo screens in a sheet-forming process that crosses fibers for strength; boiled, dried and hammered sheets become documents that survive six centuries, window paper, flooring (jangpan), lacquerware cores, layered boxes and even woven rope armor. Joseon ran state paper mills at Jeonju; twentieth-century mechanization nearly killed the craft before revival masters restarted village vats. Hanji lamp and furniture design now exports worldwide.",
